    {
      "id": "2025-2026:DATA 4300",
      "catalogYear": "2025-2026",
      "code": "DATA 4300",
      "title": "Ethics for Data Science",
      "credits": {
        "min": 3,
        "max": 3
      },
      "prerequisiteText": "Grade of C or better in DATA 3401, or department approval.",
      "prerequisites": {
        "kind": "any",
        "items": [
          {
            "kind": "course",
            "courseId": "2025-2026:DATA 3401",
            "timing": "before",
            "minimumGrade": "C"
          },
          {
            "kind": "condition",
            "category": "permission",
            "text": "Department approval"
          }
        ]
      },
      "description": "This course will introduce the student to many of the ethical issues faced by data professionals throughout the data science pipeline. Students will develop an understanding of current ethical challenges that have emerged in the profession. Topics include: bias in machine-learning algorithms and Artifical Intelligence; invasions of privacy with data collection; unintentional discrimination from data analysis; and lack of accountability with ‘black-box’ algorithms. Using philosophical ethics as a guide, the students will learn to think critically, creatively and conscientiously about how data-driven tools should be used.",
      "review": "verified",
      "sourceIds": [
        "minor-course-70418"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2025-2026:DATA 4320",
      "catalogYear": "2025-2026",
      "code": "DATA 4320",
      "title": "Advanced Machine Learning",
      "credits": {
        "min": 3,
        "max": 3
      },
      "prerequisiteText": "A grade of C or better in DATA 4419 or department approval.",
      "prerequisites": {
        "kind": "any",
        "items": [
          {
            "kind": "course",
            "courseId": "2025-2026:DATA 4419",
            "timing": "before",
            "minimumGrade": "C"
          },
          {
            "kind": "condition",
            "category": "permission",
            "text": "Department approval"
          }
        ]
      },
      "description": "This course provides an overview of theory and applications of advanced machine learning. Topics include: Neural Networks; Deep Learning models including Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN), and Generative Adversarial Networks (GAN); Attention Mechanism and Transformers; and Reinforcement Learning. Computer software tools will be used to implement machine learning models throughout the course.",
      "review": "verified",
      "sourceIds": [
        "minor-course-70555"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2025-2026:DATA 4395",
      "catalogYear": "2025-2026",
      "code": "DATA 4395",
      "title": "Senior Project in Data Science",
      "credits": {
        "min": 3,
        "max": 3
      },
      "prerequisiteText": "Senior standing, formal approval by the prospective project advisor and the department chair.",
      "prerequisites": {
        "kind": "all",
        "items": [
          {
            "kind": "condition",
            "category": "standing",
            "text": "Senior standing"
          },
          {
            "kind": "condition",
            "category": "permission",
            "text": "Formal approval by the prospective project advisor and the department chair"
          }
        ]
      },
      "description": "Crosslisted: MATH 4395 and STAT 4395. A project on a statistics related topic under the guidance of a member of the Mathematics & Statistics faculty. Students will develop writing and oral presentation skills specific to statistics by submitting a written report and giving a public presentation. Students are required to select a project advisor in advance. Individual work is accompanied by regular progress reports and classroom instruction on writing and presenting papers. Examples include research, critical literature reviews and field studies. Course can be used to fulfill the writing skills requirement.",
      "review": "verified",
      "sourceIds": [
        "minor-course-70361"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2025-2026:DATA 4419",
      "catalogYear": "2025-2026",
      "code": "DATA 4419",
      "title": "Statistical and Machine Learning",
      "credits": {
        "min": 4,
        "max": 4
      },
      "prerequisiteText": "A grade of C or better in DATA 3401; and a grade of C or better (or concurrent enrollment) in MATH 3323 and STAT 3333.",
      "prerequisites": {
        "kind": "all",
        "items": [
          {
            "kind": "course",
            "courseId": "2025-2026:DATA 3401",
            "timing": "before",
            "minimumGrade": "C"
          },
          {
            "kind": "course",
            "courseId": "2025-2026:MATH 3323",
            "timing": "before-or-concurrent",
            "minimumGrade": "C"
          },
          {
            "kind": "course",
            "courseId": "2025-2026:STAT 3333",
            "timing": "before-or-concurrent",
            "minimumGrade": "C"
          }
        ]
      },
      "description": "The course provides rigorous mathematical theory and structure for combining statistical pattern recognition with machine learning algorithms to build predictive models on a wide range of real-life data and evaluate them using a variety of appropriate metrics. Topics include, but are not limited to, supervised and unsupervised machine learning models such as linear and polynomial regression, perceptron, logistic regression, artificial neural networks, K-nearest neighbors, decision trees, support vector machines, naïve Bayes, ensemble models, principal component analysis, clustering algorithms, and association rule mining. The models will be implemented using Python programing language and Scikit-Learn, Keras, and Tensorflow libraries.",
      "review": "verified",
      "sourceIds": [
        "minor-course-70556"
      ]
    },
    {
